{"reference":"Acts 14","book":"Acts","chapter":14,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The man at Lystra","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The account introduces the man as someone who listens to Paul and then acts for himself by standing and walking. It does not assign moral fault to his disability. That focus keeps him from being treated merely as a prop in the story or as an object of shame.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:8-10"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_1852ab10bec631ce39fd2ab1"]}]},{"kind":"people_places","title":"Lystra and its crowd","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Lystra combined a Roman civic designation with continuing Lycaonian language and culture, so it should not be imagined as a culturally uniform or Latin-only city. Acts specifically marks the crowd's cry as Lycaonian; this gives the crowd a local voice and helps explain why Barnabas and Paul respond only after learning what is happening. Comparisons with regional Zeus-and-Hermes traditions make the attempted sacrifice understandable, but they do not establish a uniquely Lycaonian divine pair or verify every detail of the event.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:11-18","Acts 14:18-20","Acts 14:6-20","Acts 14:1-7","Acts 14:11-14"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_dd8360007897c68f65594275","reader_path_acts_014_21301eeac45ad3c3936d8c9f"]}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Cities and routes","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Acts securely names Derbe as a destination, but its precise site and first-century civic profile remain contested. Likewise, surviving roads and milestones show that southern provincial centers were connected, yet they cannot establish every stage, stopping place, or exact route taken by the travelers. The chapter's travel sequence is therefore concrete without being a fully recoverable itinerary.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:1-21","Acts 14:20-21"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_a6017624d6e976a5e9265609"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Violence and recovery","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The narrative moves sharply from healing and attempted sacrifice to persuasion by opponents, stoning, and departure. It does not medically describe Paul's recovery, so his survival should not be used to minimize injury or to promise an identical recovery for others. Acts and Paul's letters are distinct witnesses; this episode should not be used to diagnose or harmonize every bodily reference in those letters.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:8-20"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_5fdf976af295293b431a291f"]}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"The return journey","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The return through Lystra, Iconium, and Antioch is more than retracing a route. Acts presents strengthening the disciples, prayer and fasting, and the appointment of elders in each assembly before Barnabas and Paul report back to the community that sent them. This highlights repeated contact and local leadership, while the compact account should not be turned into a complete constitution for these congregations.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:21-26","Acts 14:21-28"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_c3795dded9367d582cfcc13b","reader_path_acts_014_5c9842adc2c9dba2c4aff6df"]}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"A later recollection","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Another letter recalls persecutions at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra and credits the Lord with rescue. That recollection places the chapter's violence within a remembered sequence of opposition across these cities.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:19-22"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_6ec2a2a748d86d4170f604de"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts alongside other sources","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"Acts supplies a named sequence of southern cities and regional movements, while Galatians gives selective first-person movements, Jerusalem meetings, an Antioch conflict, and an unnamed Galatian audience. The two accounts can be compared, but neither should be used to fill every silence in the other.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:13-52","Acts 15:1-41","Acts 16:1-6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_82ba95f9481312ee69aef2d4"]},{"id":"block_8","text":"Questions about Acts as history are best considered claim by claim, comparing particular details with independent evidence rather than treating the entire book as either simply reliable or simply unreliable.","verse_refs":["Acts 10:1-48","Acts 11:1-30","Acts 12:1-25","Acts 13:1-52","Acts 15:1-41","Acts 16:1-40","Acts 17:1-34","Acts 18:1-28","Acts 19:1-41","Acts 1:1-26","Acts 20:1-38","Acts 21:1-40","Acts 22:1-30","Acts 23:1-35","Acts 24:1-27","Acts 25:1-27","Acts 26:1-32","Acts 27:1-44","Acts 28:1-31","Acts 2:1-47","Acts 3:1-26","Acts 4:1-37","Acts 5:1-42","Acts 6:1-15","Acts 7:1-60","Acts 8:1-40","Acts 9:1-43"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_014_a707229eef89fc8fc6cbae12"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}